  "writeup_markdown": "---\ntitle: \"Idoma\"\nsubtitle: \"Nigeria\"\nregion: \"Sub Saharan Africa\"\ntags: [ethnography, sub-saharan-africa]\n---\n\n## At a glance\n| | |\n|---|---|\n| Who | Idoma communities of central Nigeria. |\n| Where | Mainly Benue State and neighboring areas. |\n| Known for | Carved masks and headdresses ? Figurative sculpture ? Barkcloth dress ? Metalwork |\n\n## Overview\n\nThe Idoma live in central Nigeria, especially the Benue region. This collection is based on objects that museums explicitly attribute to Idoma makers. It includes painted wooden face masks, a sculptural headdress, a seated mother-and-child figure, a barkcloth dress, carved stools, a brass bell, and ironwork. The range matters: masks are prominent, but they are only part of the material record.\n\n## Material culture\n\nThe British Museum's early twentieth-century holdings show several distinct face masks. Some are white or black-and-white; others have horns, beards, carved facial markings, or metal teeth. One headdress carries two human figures and a patterned animal. The Smithsonian notes that related masks moved across ethnic boundaries and associations, and that the function of a particular mask cannot always be established from its appearance. These objects should therefore not all be assigned one ceremony.\n\nThe same British Museum collection records a painted, fringed barkcloth dress and a vegetable-fibre apron with seeds. Two carved wooden stools have different support structures. A cast brass bell, decorated arm ring, and iron arrowhead show additional uses of metal. These are documented individual objects, not a complete account of Idoma dress or daily life.\n\nThe Commons photographs selected for this page show museum objects and contemporary cultural performances. Their captions identify Idoma people or makers, but a photograph alone cannot establish the age or customary use of every form shown.\n\n## Sources & further reading\n\n- [British Museum: black-and-white Idoma mask](https://www.britishmuseum.org/collection/object/E_Af1920-1011-28) ? maker attribution, date, and collection history.\n- [British Museum: Idoma headdress](https://www.britishmuseum.org/collection/object/E_Af1972-18-1) ? carved figures and animal design.\n- [British Museum: barkcloth dress](https://www.britishmuseum.org/collection/object/E_Af1920-1011-41) ? material and painted decoration.\n- [British Museum: wooden stool](https://www.britishmuseum.org/collection/object/E_Af1913-1013-2) ? construction and attribution.\n- [Smithsonian National Museum of African Art: Idoma face mask](https://africa.si.edu/collection/object/nmafa_2005-6-88) ? cautions about assigning mask use and ethnic boundaries.\n",
